1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:07,228
When you apply for a mortgage, someone at the bank goes through two months of your statements, line by line.

2
00:00:07,228 --> 00:00:11,240
A big deposit they can't trace, an overdraft fee, a casino bet.

3
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:15,380
Most people only find out what got flagged after they've applied.

4
00:00:16,180 --> 00:00:21,213
So I built Underwriter's Eye. It does the same review in Chrome, before you apply.

5
00:00:21,213 --> 00:00:26,184
Gemini Nano reads the statements on your own computer, through Firebase AI Logic.

6
00:00:26,184 --> 00:00:29,130
The cloud model only gets a few rounded numbers.

7
00:00:29,930 --> 00:00:32,920
Here's Maya, a sample applicant in Austin.

8
00:00:32,920 --> 00:00:38,973
My rules handle most of her lines, and Nano picks up the eleven they don't recognise.

9
00:00:38,973 --> 00:00:42,960
With real statements, that part never leaves the device.

10
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:48,937
If a line could change the outcome and Nano isn't sure, the app just asks.

11
00:00:48,937 --> 00:00:52,225
Here it thought this $6,000 deposit was income.

12
00:00:52,225 --> 00:00:55,093
I don't let a model's guess clear a flag,

13
00:00:55,093 --> 00:00:59,430
so it stays flagged until Maya says where the money came from.

14
00:01:00,230 --> 00:01:03,796
Now the report, the way an underwriter would see it.

15
00:01:03,796 --> 00:01:07,088
One thing to fix, four things they'll ask about.

16
00:01:07,088 --> 00:01:11,340
The slider shows how her debt ratio moves with a cheaper home.

17
00:01:12,140 --> 00:01:15,203
The 90-day plan is worked out on the device:

18
00:01:15,203 --> 00:01:18,475
cut $141 a month, or keep housing under $1,700.

19
00:01:18,475 --> 00:01:23,140
Gemini in the cloud can reword it, but it never sees those numbers,

20
00:01:23,140 --> 00:01:26,690
and if it writes a digit, I throw the wording away.

21
00:01:27,490 --> 00:01:34,440
The cloud model also asks the questions an underwriter would probably ask, from the summary alone.

22
00:01:35,240 --> 00:01:37,454
Maya's answers stay on the laptop.

23
00:01:37,454 --> 00:01:43,444
Nano turns them into a paragraph, and the code checks it didn't add anything she didn't say.

24
00:01:43,444 --> 00:01:49,630
The letter template comes from the cloud with blanks, and names and amounts are filled in here.

25
00:01:50,430 --> 00:01:53,631
She can also just ask her statements a question.

26
00:01:53,631 --> 00:01:57,700
Nano works out what she means, and the app does the counting.

27
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:01,180
If you don't want to take my word for it,

28
00:02:01,180 --> 00:02:05,560
this panel shows every request that left the device, byte for byte.

29
00:02:05,560 --> 00:02:11,770
App Check protects the calls, and Remote Config lets me switch the cloud off without an update.

30
00:02:12,570 --> 00:02:17,280
Two months later, Maya runs it again. Everything's green.

31
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:25,430
That's Underwriter's Eye. It's live at underwriters-eye.web.app, and the code is on GitHub.
